            <section class="research-hero"><div><p class="eyebrow">Composite Fabrication · Materials Testing</p><h1>Hybrid Fiber Composites</h1><p class="subtitle">Mechanical Performance of Jute, Glass, and Hybrid Laminates</p><p class="hero-intro">Experimental research comparing natural, synthetic, and hybrid reinforcement in a biobased epoxy matrix through specimen fabrication and mechanical characterization.</p><div class="research-meta"><span>Tuskegee University</span><span>Jan. – May 2023</span><span>Research assistant</span></div><p class="status">Collaborative materials research</p><div class="hero-actions"><a class="button primary" href="#contributions">My contributions ↓</a><a class="button" href="#results">Explore the test results ↓</a></div></div><figure class="specimen-hero"><a href="/images/research/hybrid-composites/composite-specimens.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label="Open full figure: Three composite systems"><img src="/images/research/hybrid-composites/composite-specimens.jpeg" alt="Photograph of jute, glass, and hybrid composite specimens labeled in the research presentation" width="657" height="879" /></a><figcaption><strong>Three composite systems</strong><span>Jute, glass, and hybrid specimens shown in the Spring 2023 presentation.</span></figcaption></figure></section>
            <nav class="page-index" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#question">Research question</a><a href="#contributions">My work</a><a href="#methods">Fabrication &amp; tests</a><a href="#results">Results</a><a href="#takeaways">Takeaways</a></nav>
            <section class="section split" id="question"><div><p class="eyebrow">01</p><h2>Combine Natural and Synthetic Reinforcement</h2></div><div class="copy"><p class="lead">How does adding glass fiber change the mechanical behavior of a jute composite?</p><p>The study compared jute, E-glass, and hybrid jute/glass fiber-reinforced composites. Each system used a biobased epoxy matrix, allowing us to examine the effect of reinforcement on strength, stiffness, and damping behavior.</p><p>The motivation was to improve the mechanical performance of a natural-fiber composite by combining it with synthetic reinforcement.</p></div></section>
            <section class="section dark" id="contributions"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">02</p><h2>My Contributions</h2></div><p>I participated in fabrication, testing, and analysis with the research team.</p></div><div class="contribution-list"><div><span>01</span><p>Fabricated composite laminates using wet hand lay-up and compression molding with a biobased epoxy matrix.</p></div><div><span>02</span><p>Prepared specimens and performed tensile, flexural, and dynamic mechanical testing across the three composite systems.</p></div><div><span>03</span><p>Compared mechanical strength and DMA responses, including storage modulus, loss modulus, and tan delta.</p></div><div><span>04</span><p>Coauthored the project report and presented the fabrication methods, test results, and directions for further study.</p></div></div></section>
            <section class="section" id="methods"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">03</p><h2>Fabrication and Characterization</h2></div><p>Three reinforcement systems followed the same overall fabrication and testing workflow.</p></div><div class="method-grid"><article><p class="step-number">01</p><h3>Fabricate laminates</h3><p>Prepare jute, E-glass, and hybrid laminates through wet hand lay-up and compression molding.</p></article><article><p class="step-number">02</p><h3>Prepare specimens</h3><p>Cut smaller specimens from the completed composite laminates for mechanical and dynamic testing.</p></article><article><p class="step-number">03</p><h3>Compare responses</h3><p>Use tensile and flexural tests for strength, then DMA to examine stiffness and damping as temperature changes.</p></article></div><table><caption>What the tests reveal</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Test</th><th scope="col">Response studied</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Tensile</th><td>Strength under axial loading</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Flexural</th><td>Strength under bending</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">DMA</th><td>Temperature-dependent storage modulus, loss modulus, and tan delta</td></tr></tbody></table></section>
            <section class="section" id="results"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">04</p><h2>Hybridization Improved on Jute Alone</h2></div><p>The detailed presentation results place the hybrid composite between the jute and glass systems for tensile and flexural strength.</p></div><table><caption>Qualitative comparisons reported in the presentation</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Property</th><th scope="col">Reported ordering</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Tensile strength</th><td>Glass &gt; Hybrid &gt; Jute</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Flexural strength</th><td>Glass &gt; Hybrid &gt; Jute</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Storage and loss modulus</th><td>Glass highest, jute lowest</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Tan delta</th><td>Hybrid highest, close to glass; jute lowest</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="dma-gallery" style="margin-top: 3rem"><figure class=""><a href="/images/research/hybrid-composites/jute-dma.png" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label="Open full figure: Jute composite"><img src="/images/research/hybrid-composites/jute-dma.png" alt="Original jute-composite DMA plot with storage modulus, loss modulus, and tan delta versus temperature" width="586" height="450" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><strong>Jute composite</strong><span>Original DMA plot from the study presentation.</span></figcaption></figure><figure class=""><a href="/images/research/hybrid-composites/glass-dma.png" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label="Open full figure: E-glass composite"><img src="/images/research/hybrid-composites/glass-dma.png" alt="Original E-glass-composite DMA plot with storage modulus, loss modulus, and tan delta versus temperature" width="587" height="450" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><strong>E-glass composite</strong><span>Original DMA plot from the study presentation.</span></figcaption></figure><figure class=""><a href="/images/research/hybrid-composites/hybrid-dma.png" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label="Open full figure: Hybrid jute/glass composite"><img src="/images/research/hybrid-composites/hybrid-dma.png" alt="Original hybrid jute and E-glass DMA plot with storage modulus, loss modulus, and tan delta versus temperature" width="585" height="450" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><strong>Hybrid jute/glass composite</strong><span>Original DMA plot from the study presentation.</span></figcaption></figure></div><p class="media-note">Original DMA plots from the Spring 2023 presentation. Each plot uses its own axis ranges. Select a figure for full resolution.</p></section>
            <section class="section split" id="takeaways"><div><p class="eyebrow">05</p><h2>Reinforcement Changes the Tradeoff</h2></div><div class="copy"><p>The hybrid system improved mechanical performance relative to the jute-only composite while retaining natural-fiber reinforcement. The E-glass system remained the strongest in the tensile and flexural comparisons reported in the presentation.</p><p>The work connected material selection and laminate fabrication to measured behavior. A proposed next step was to investigate how the stacking order of jute and glass layers affects the hybrid response.</p><p class="credits"><strong>Presentation authors:</strong> Daniel Winfield, Habeeb Shahan, and Walter Daniels. The presentation acknowledges Dr. Zainuddin, Richard Harry, mentor Habeeb Shahan, and Walter Daniels.</p></div></section>

